<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">San José de Oruña</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1592</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as St. Joseph, Trinidad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San José de Oruña, now St. Joseph, was the first permanent Spanish town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, founded in 1592 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-berrio">Antonio de Berrío</a> inland from the Gulf of Paria as a base for his search for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Walter Raleigh</a> attacked and burned it in 1595. It remained the capital of the colony until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-spain">Port of Spain</a> took its place in the eighteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-jose-de-oruna.jpg" alt="San José de Oruña" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grueslayer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TnT_St._Joseph_Mohammed_Ali_Jinnah_Memorial_Mosque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
